(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["anatomy-exam-3-male-reproductive-quiz"]={"title":"Male Reproductive System — Anatomy Exam 3","category":"Anatomy","exam":"Exam 3","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"During development, the testes first form in which location before descending?","choices":["Inside the abdominal cavity near the kidneys","Inside the fully descended scrotum","Within the traversed inguinal canal","Within the pelvic cavity near the bladder"],"answer":0,"exp":"The testes form inside the abdominal cavity near the kidneys during development, then descend toward the scrotum. — Slide 8 — Descent of the Testes"},{"q":"Which structure holds the testes in place and, by failing to elongate as the body grows, pulls them inferiorly toward the scrotum?","choices":["The full spermatic cord","The gubernaculum testis","The tunica vaginalis","The cremaster muscle"],"answer":1,"exp":"The gubernaculum testis holds the testes in place and, because it does not elongate with body growth, pulls the testes inferiorly toward the scrotum. — Slide 8 — Gubernaculum testis"},{"q":"As the testes descend, they reach the scrotum by passing through which structure?","choices":["The obturator foramen","The femoral canal","The inguinal canals","The urogenital diaphragm"],"answer":2,"exp":"The testes descend toward the scrotum by passing through the inguinal canals. — Slide 8 — pass through the inguinal canals"},{"q":"After descent, which structures remain bundled to the testes within the spermatic cords?","choices":["The ureter and full urinary bladder together","The corpora cavernosa and corpus spongiosum","The prostate gland and paired seminal glands","The ductus deferens, blood vessels, lymphatics, and nerves"],"answer":3,"exp":"The ductus deferens, blood vessels, lymphatics, and nerves remain bundled to the testes in the spermatic cords. — Slide 8 — spermatic cords"},{"q":"According to the lecture, why do the testes develop within the abdominal cavity but sperm develop outside it in the scrotum?","choices":["Testicle development needs ~98.6°F while sperm development needs a cooler ~96.6°F","Sperm require a warmer temperature than the testes do, near 100°F","The scrotum provides substantially more physical space for testicular growth","Abdominal pressure is specifically required for normal sperm formation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Testicle development requires about 98.6°F (hence within the abdomen), while sperm development requires a cooler ~96.6°F (hence outside the abdomen in the scrotum). — Slide 9 — temperature requirements for testicle vs sperm development"},{"q":"Powerful contractions of which two muscles push semen toward the external urethral orifice during ejaculation?","choices":["Dartos and cremaster muscles","Ischiocavernosus and bulbospongiosus","Rectus abdominis and pyramidalis","Levator ani and coccygeus muscles"],"answer":1,"exp":"Ejaculation involves powerful contractions of the ischiocavernosus and bulbospongiosus muscles, which push semen toward the external urethral orifice. — Slide 66 — Ejaculation: contractions of ischiocavernosus and bulbospongiosus"},{"q":"The spongy urethra passes through which muscle at the base of the penis, as labeled in the reproductive system figure?","choices":["Cremaster muscle","Dartos muscle","Bulbospongiosus","Ischiocavernosus"],"answer":2,"exp":"In the figure, the spongy (penile) urethra and the bulb of the penis are associated with the bulbospongiosus muscle. — Slide 48/51 — Figure 27.1 (Bulbospongiosus)"},{"q":"Which superficial smooth muscle of the scrotum contracts to wrinkle the scrotal surface?","choices":["Cremaster muscle","Ischiocavernosus","Bulbospongiosus","Dartos muscle"],"answer":3,"exp":"The dartos muscle is a superficial smooth muscle whose contraction causes wrinkling of the scrotal surface. — Slide 17 — Dartos muscle"},{"q":"The internal pudendal arteries that help supply the scrotum are branches of which vessel?","choices":["The internal iliac artery","The femoral artery","The external iliac artery","The abdominal aorta"],"answer":0,"exp":"The internal pudendal arteries are branches of the internal iliac artery. — Slide 20 — Internal pudendal arteries from internal iliac"},{"q":"The external pudendal arteries supplying the scrotum arise from which vessel?","choices":["The internal iliac artery","The femoral artery","The external iliac artery","The testicular artery"],"answer":1,"exp":"The external pudendal arteries are branches of the femoral artery. — Slide 20 — External pudendal arteries from femoral artery"},{"q":"The inferior epigastric arteries that help vascularize the scrotum branch from which vessel?","choices":["The femoral artery","The internal iliac artery","The external iliac artery","The internal pudendal artery"],"answer":2,"exp":"The inferior epigastric arteries are branches of the external iliac artery. — Slide 20 — Inferior epigastric arteries from external iliac"},{"q":"Which two arteries are named as blood vessels running within the spermatic cord?","choices":["Superior and inferior vesical arteries","Internal and external pudendal arteries","Deep and dorsal arteries of the penis","Deferential and testicular arteries"],"answer":3,"exp":"The spermatic cord contains the deferential artery and the testicular artery (along with the pampiniform plexus of the testicular vein). — Slide 13 — Spermatic cord: deferential artery, testicular artery"},{"q":"The network of veins in the spermatic cord that drains the testis is called the:","choices":["Pampiniform plexus","Hypogastric plexus","Prostatic plexus","Dorsal venous plexus"],"answer":0,"exp":"The pampiniform plexus of the testicular vein is the venous network within the spermatic cord. — Slide 13 — Pampiniform plexus of the testicular vein"},{"q":"Which artery lies in the center of each corpus cavernosum of the penis?","choices":["The dorsal artery of the penis","The deep artery of the penis","The internal pudendal artery","The deferential artery"],"answer":1,"exp":"The deep artery of the penis lies in the center of the corpus cavernosum. — Slide 61, 64 — Deep artery of the penis in the corpora cavernosa"},{"q":"Which two nerves are named as running within the spermatic cord?","choices":["Pudendal and obturator nerves","Femoral and sciatic nerves of the leg","Ilioinguinal and genitofemoral nerves","Hypogastric and vagus nerves centrally"],"answer":2,"exp":"The ilioinguinal nerve and the genitofemoral nerve are the nerves named within the spermatic cord. — Slide 13 — Spermatic cord nerves: ilioinguinal, genitofemoral"},{"q":"According to the lecture, the scrotum is supplied with sensory and motor nerves from all of the following EXCEPT:","choices":["The hypogastric plexus","The ilioinguinal nerves","The pudendal nerves","The vagus nerve"],"answer":3,"exp":"The scrotum is supplied by the hypogastric plexus and the ilioinguinal, genitofemoral, and pudendal nerves — the vagus nerve is not listed. — Slide 21 — Scrotal innervation"},{"q":"Erection of the penis is initiated by activation of which type of nerves?","choices":["Parasympathetic nerves","Sympathetic nerves","Somatic motor nerves only","The vagus nerve"],"answer":0,"exp":"Erection begins when parasympathetic nerves are activated, causing arterial smooth muscle to relax and the erectile tissue to engorge with blood. — Slide 65 — Erection: parasympathetic nerves activated"},{"q":"During semen release, which division of the autonomic nervous system controls emission?","choices":["Parasympathetic activity","Sympathetic activity","Enteric activity","Somatic reflex only"],"answer":1,"exp":"Emission is driven by sympathetic activity, mixing sperm with semen via peristaltic smooth-muscle action in the ductus deferens, seminal glands, prostate, and bulbo-urethral glands. — Slide 66 — Emission: sympathetic activity"},{"q":"The reproductive system is organized into gonads, a reproductive tract, accessory glands, and external genitalia. In the male, the gonads are the:","choices":["Epididymides","Seminal glands","Testes","Prostate"],"answer":2,"exp":"The gonads are the testes, which produce gametes (sperm) and hormones. — Slide 3 — Organization of the Reproductive System"},{"q":"The two scrotal chambers are separated by which structure, seen as thickened tissue extending from the anus across the scrotum?","choices":["The scrotal septum","The tunica albuginea","The dartos muscle fascia","The raphe of the scrotum"],"answer":3,"exp":"The raphe of the scrotum separates the two scrotal cavities and appears as thickened tissue extending from the anus, across the scrotum, and along the anterior penis. — Slide 15 — Raphe of the scrotum"},{"q":"Which serous membrane lines each scrotal cavity and reduces friction between its opposing layers?","choices":["Tunica vaginalis","Tunica albuginea","Dartos fascia","Cremasteric fascia"],"answer":0,"exp":"The tunica vaginalis is a serous membrane that lines each scrotal cavity and reduces friction between the opposing serous layers. — Slide 15 — Tunica vaginalis lines each cavity"},{"q":"Contraction of the cremaster muscle produces which effect?","choices":["Wrinkles the scrotal skin via smooth muscle","Tenses the scrotal sac and pulls it closer to the body","Relaxes arterial smooth muscle walls within the penis","Propels sperm forward into the urethral lumen"],"answer":1,"exp":"Cremaster contraction tenses the scrotal sac and pulls it closer to the body to help maintain the proper (cooler) temperature for sperm development. — Slide 18 — Cremaster muscle"},{"q":"The fibrous connective-tissue capsule of the testis that extends inward to form septa is the:","choices":["Tunica vaginalis","Mediastinum testis","Tunica albuginea","Rete testis"],"answer":2,"exp":"The tunica albuginea is the fibrous connective-tissue lining that extends deep into the testis and forms septa. — Slide 22 — Tunica albuginea forms septa"},{"q":"The septa of the testis converge toward which region, which supports the ducts moving material toward the epididymis?","choices":["The efferent ductule pathway","The rete testis chamber","The testicular lobule wall","The mediastinum of the testis"],"answer":3,"exp":"The septa converge toward the mediastinum of the testis, which supports the ducts that move material toward the epididymis. — Slide 22 — Mediastinum of the testis"},{"q":"Each testicular lobule contains tightly coiled structures where sperm production begins. These are the:","choices":["Seminiferous tubules","Straight tubules","Efferent ductules","Rete testis channels"],"answer":0,"exp":"Each lobule contains tightly coiled seminiferous tubules (each ~80 cm long) where spermatogenesis begins. — Slide 24 — Seminiferous tubules"},{"q":"Place the following in the correct order of sperm flow within the testis: seminiferous tubules → ___ → rete testis → efferent ductules.","choices":["Ejaculatory duct","Straight tubules","Ductus deferens","Ampulla"],"answer":1,"exp":"Seminiferous tubules uncoil into straight tubules, which interconnect to form the rete testis, which connects to the efferent ductules leading to the epididymis. — Slide 24 — Straight tubules → rete testis → efferent ductules"},{"q":"The interstitial endocrine cells (Leydig cells) located between the seminiferous tubules produce which hormone?","choices":["Inhibin","Androgen-binding protein","Testosterone","Luteinizing hormone"],"answer":2,"exp":"The interstitial endocrine cells (Leydig cells) produce and release testosterone. — Slide 26 — Interstitial endocrine cells (Leydig cells) produce testosterone"},{"q":"Nurse cells (Sertoli cells) maintain the blood-testis barrier, which functions to:","choices":["Substantially increase arterial blood flow directly to the testicular parenchyma","Secrete testosterone directly into the systemic bloodstream for distribution","Store mature sperm within the seminiferous tubular lumen prior to release","Isolate the seminiferous tubules from circulation to prevent autoimmune destruction of sperm"],"answer":3,"exp":"The blood-testis barrier isolates the seminiferous tubules from circulation to prevent autoimmune destruction of sperm cells. — Slide 35 — Nurse cells maintain the blood-testis barrier"},{"q":"Which two substances are secreted by the nurse (Sertoli) cells?","choices":["Inhibin and androgen-binding protein (ABP)","Fructose and clotting-factor proteins","Testosterone and androstenedione","Zinc and citric acid from the prostate"],"answer":0,"exp":"Nurse cells secrete inhibin (which controls the rate of sperm formation) and androgen-binding protein (ABP), which keeps testosterone active within the seminiferous tubules. — Slide 35 — Nurse cells secrete inhibin and ABP"},{"q":"A sperm cell's head contains chromosomes and an acrosome. The acrosome enzyme necessary for fertilization of the egg is:","choices":["Seminalplasmin","Hyaluronidase","Fructose","Inhibin"],"answer":1,"exp":"The acrosome contains enzymes including hyaluronidase, which is necessary for fertilization of the egg. — Slide 37 — Acrosome enzyme hyaluronidase"},{"q":"The middle piece of a sperm cell contains which organelles that power its movement?","choices":["Ribosomes and lysosomes","Golgi apparatus and nucleus","Mitochondria and centrioles","Acrosome and chromosomes"],"answer":2,"exp":"The middle piece (with the neck) contains mitochondria and centrioles. — Slide 37 — Middle piece: mitochondria and centrioles"},{"q":"The epididymis is divided into three parts. In which part do the coiled tubes begin to uncoil to become the ductus deferens?","choices":["Head","Body","Ampulla","Tail"],"answer":3,"exp":"In the tail of the epididymis, the tubes begin to uncoil to become the single ductus deferens. — Slide 41 — Epididymis: head, body, tail"},{"q":"Which statement about the functions of the epididymis is correct?","choices":["It stores spermatozoa and serves as a recycling center for damaged sperm","It produces roughly 60% of total semen volume from accessory glands","It secretes testosterone directly into the systemic bloodstream for use","It is the normal anatomical site where fertilization actually takes place"],"answer":0,"exp":"The epididymis stores spermatozoa for further maturation and acts as a recycling center for damaged spermatozoa (sperm take ~2 weeks to pass through). — Slide 43 — Functions of the epididymis"},{"q":"The penis consists of three cylindrical columns of erectile tissue. These are:","choices":["Three corpora cavernosa","Two corpora cavernosa and one corpus spongiosum","Two corpora spongiosa and one corpus cavernosum","One corpus cavernosum and one corpus spongiosum"],"answer":1,"exp":"The penis has two posterior corpora cavernosa and one anterior corpus spongiosum. — Slide 60 — erectile tissue columns"},{"q":"The corpus spongiosum of the penis surrounds the spongy urethra and its thickened proximal end forms the:","choices":["Crus of the penis","Glans penis","Bulb of the penis","Prepuce"],"answer":2,"exp":"The corpus spongiosum surrounds the spongy urethra, and its thickened proximal end forms the bulb of the penis. — Slide 61 — corpus spongiosum forms the bulb of the penis"},{"q":"Spermatogenesis (sperm formation) begins in which layer of the seminiferous tubules?","choices":["The lumen","The rete testis","The mediastinum","The outer layer"],"answer":3,"exp":"Spermatogenesis begins in the outer layer of the seminiferous tubules, where spermatogonia (stem cells) reside. — Slide 29 — spermatogenesis begins in the outer layer"},{"q":"A single primary spermatocyte undergoing meiosis gives rise to how many sperm cells?","choices":["Four","Two","One","Twenty-three"],"answer":0,"exp":"A single primary spermatocyte gives rise to a total of four sperm cells (via meiosis I → two secondary spermatocytes, then meiosis II → four spermatids). — Slide 31 — one primary spermatocyte → four sperm cells"},{"q":"The physical maturation of spermatids into sperm cells, during which they are embedded in nurse cells, is called:","choices":["Spermatogenesis","Spermiogenesis","Capacitation","Emission"],"answer":1,"exp":"Spermiogenesis is the maturation of spermatids into physically mature sperm cells while embedded in nurse (Sertoli) cells. — Slide 34 — Spermiogenesis"},{"q":"After leaving the testis, sperm are physically but not functionally mature. Where are they stored and monitored for further maturation?","choices":["The seminal gland","The prostate","The epididymis","The ejaculatory duct"],"answer":2,"exp":"The epididymis stores sperm for further maturation and monitors the fluid from the seminiferous tubules; sperm take ~2 weeks to pass through. — Slide 43 — epididymis stores spermatozoa"},{"q":"Before entering the prostate gland, the ductus deferens expands into a region called the:","choices":["Bulb","Rete testis","Efferent ductule","Ampulla"],"answer":3,"exp":"Before entering the prostate, the ductus deferens expands into the ampulla, which joins the excretory duct of the seminal gland to become the ejaculatory duct. — Slide 47 — ductus deferens expands into the ampulla"},{"q":"The ampulla of the ductus deferens joins the excretory duct of the seminal gland to form which structure?","choices":["The ejaculatory duct","The membranous urethra","The efferent ductule","The prostatic ductule"],"answer":0,"exp":"The ampulla joins the excretory duct of the seminal gland to become the ejaculatory duct, which empties into the prostatic urethra. — Slide 47 — ejaculatory duct formation"},{"q":"The ejaculatory duct empties into which part of the urethra?","choices":["The membranous urethra","The prostatic urethra","The spongy urethra","The external urethral orifice"],"answer":1,"exp":"The ejaculatory duct empties into the prostatic urethra. — Slide 47 — ejaculatory duct empties into the prostatic urethra"},{"q":"During emission, sperm are mixed with semen by peristaltic smooth-muscle action in all of the following EXCEPT:","choices":["The ductus deferens","The seminal glands","The epididymis head only","The prostate and bulbo-urethral glands"],"answer":2,"exp":"Emission mixes sperm with semen by peristalsis in the ductus deferens, seminal glands, prostate, and bulbo-urethral glands — not by the epididymis head alone. — Slide 66 — Emission structures"},{"q":"Mature sperm cells become motile only after undergoing which process, which occurs outside the epididymis?","choices":["Spermiogenesis","Emission","Meiosis","Capacitation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Mature sperm are not motile until they undergo capacitation, which happens outside the epididymis (motility begins when mixed with seminal gland secretions). — Slide 43–44 — capacitation"},{"q":"Which accessory gland produces about 60 percent of the semen volume, with fluid rich in fructose, prostaglandins, and clotting factors?","choices":["The seminal glands","The prostate","The bulbo-urethral glands","The epididymis"],"answer":0,"exp":"The seminal glands (seminal vesicles) produce about 60% of the semen volume, with a high concentration of fructose, prostaglandins, and clotting factors. — Slide 54 — The Seminal Glands"},{"q":"The prostate secretes a weakly acidic fluid making up 20–30% of semen. It contains seminalplasmin, which functions as:","choices":["The principal energy source fueling sperm motility","An antibiotic believed to prevent UTIs in males","A factor that coagulates semen after ejaculation","An alkaline lubricant secreted at the penis tip"],"answer":1,"exp":"Seminalplasmin is an antibiotic believed to prevent urinary tract infections in males; prostatic secretions also contain enzymes that prevent sperm coagulation in the vagina. — Slide 55 — The Prostate: seminalplasmin"},{"q":"The bulbo-urethral (Cowper's) glands produce ~5% of semen volume. Their thick, sticky, alkaline mucus empties into which structure and serves what purpose?","choices":["The prostatic urethra; to coagulate semen immediately after ejaculation for transport","The ejaculatory duct; to provide fructose as an energy substrate for sperm cells","The spongy urethra; to neutralize residual urinary acids and lubricate the penis tip","The ampulla of the ductus deferens; to store sperm prior to ejaculation occurring"],"answer":2,"exp":"The bulbo-urethral glands empty into the spongy urethra (just distal to the body wall); their alkaline mucus neutralizes residual urinary acids and lubricates the tip of the penis. — Slide 56 — The Bulbo-urethral Glands"},{"q":"The male urethra is divided into three regions. Which region extends from the urinary bladder through the prostate gland?","choices":["Ejaculatory urethra","Membranous urethra","Spongy (penile) urethra","Prostatic urethra"],"answer":3,"exp":"The prostatic urethra extends from the urinary bladder through the prostate gland. — Slide 50 — Prostatic urethra"},{"q":"Which region of the urethra passes through the muscular wall of the pelvic cavity, between the prostatic and spongy portions?","choices":["Membranous urethra","Prostatic urethra","Spongy urethra","Cavernous urethra"],"answer":0,"exp":"The membranous urethra passes through the muscular wall of the pelvic cavity. — Slide 50 — Membranous urethra"},{"q":"The spongy urethra, sometimes called the penile urethra, passes through which structure?","choices":["The prostate gland","The penis","The pelvic diaphragm","The seminal gland"],"answer":1,"exp":"The spongy (penile) urethra passes through the penis, within the corpus spongiosum. — Slide 50 — Spongy (penile) urethra"}]};
